// #259 regression pin: a surface-and-stop path in `jit_call_value` must not
// hand back a legal value.
//
// The callee `g` is stamped `UInt64` (the `MirConstant::Function` carrier kind)
// but its bits are neither an inline function nor a unified-heap closure, so
// `jit_call_value` correctly detects a carrier violation at
// `crates/shape-jit/src/ffi/control/mod.rs` (the UInt64 callee arm). Before
// #259 it then returned `TAG_NULL` — which read as `i64` is
// -1407374883553280 — and the caller printed it as an ordinary integer, rc=0.
//
// No arithmetic on the result, deliberately: a regression shows up as the raw
// TAG_NULL word rather than as an off-by-one, so the failure names its own
// cause.
//
// NOW EXECUTES NATIVELY (2026-08-03, #239 §6.7 forward closure-callee
// resolution). `h`'s only capture is `g`, and `g` is built at a single site
// from a closure constant, so `mir_compiler::closure_callee` resolves the
// indirect callee inside `h` and stamps the call destination from `g`'s entry
// in the same return table a direct call uses. The §4.1 return-ABI class can
// then be selected, the whole program compiles, and `--mode jit` prints 2 with
// 3 native dispatches instead of deopting.
//
// Read the acceptance gate, not the differential, for this program: it was
// already classified MATCH while the JIT was inert (both tiers ran the
// interpreter and agreed trivially — #260 vacuity), so the differential cannot
// see the difference. `scripts/check-jit-native-acceptance.sh` can.
//
// Still pinned in known-red.json as `callvalue-stop-aborts-native`; that class
// retires on its own terms, not this lane's. If this program ever prints a
// number under `--mode jit` that is not 2, the stop has started returning a
// value again — or the callee resolution stamped the wrong kind.
let c = 0
let g = |x| x + 1 + c
let h = |y| g(y)
print(h(1))
